    "asset_id": "AST_e5909c26ab6c",
    "modality": "pdf",
    "text": "Description (Background, Detailed Description and Expected Solution): \nBackground: \nFree Space Optical Communication (FSOC) offers unprecedented advantages for next-generation mobile \nnetworks, including gigabit-to-terabit data rates, license-free spectrum operation, high immunity to \nelectromagnetic interference, etc. However, deploying FSOC links between mobile platforms (satellites, UAVs \npresents a severe challenge of pointing, acquisition and tracking (PAT) of highly narrow laser beams. PAT \ntypically happens in two stages: coarse alignment and fine alignment. Coarse alignment is one of the key \nchallenges of PAT, where the transmitting terminal must first locate and maintain the remote terminal within its \ncamera Field-of-View (FOV). \nDeveloping and testing such algorithms on real hardware requires expensive cameras, pan-tilt mechanisms, and \noptical components & equipment. A software based virtual camera tracking provides an inexpensive and \naccessible platform for algorithm development and learning. \nDescription: \nUnlike conventional radio-frequency systems, FSOC relies on a highly directional optical beam. Even a small \nangular error can prevent successful communication. Before fine pointing mechanism can take over, a coarse \nalignment stage must:",
    "visual_description": null,
    "page_number": 1,
    "slide_number": null,
    "timestamp_start": null,
    "timestamp_end": null,
    "frame_id": null,
    "sequence_index": 1,
    "chapter": null,
    "section": null,
    "parent_content_id": null,
    "char_start": null,
    "char_end": null,
